  "anchor2": {
   "name": "industry/USGS silicon-metal consumption (China ~55-60%)",
   "independent": false,
   "note": "the China anchor is a 5-point RANGE (~55-60%), not a point \u2014 almost any plausible build lands inside a 5-point band, so it cannot discriminate a good number from a mediocre one; and USGS is our production input, so there is still no second source with a different upstream. A POINT anchor (a national statistics office publishing silicon consumption, or an independently-built Eurostat PRODCOM EU figure) is what would move silicon toward A.",
   "kind": "no-study-group"
  },
  "of_what": "METALLURGICAL silicon metal (HS 280469, <99.99% Si) apparent consumption = production + imports - exports. NOT polysilicon (HS 280461, the solar chain) and NOT semiconductors; silicon metal goes mostly to aluminium alloying, silicones, then polysilicon feedstock. Refined absorption only; 2020 snapshot (USGS MYB silicon lags and the industry has grown since); assumes zero stock change.",
  "hs_note": "280469 (<99.99% Si) bundles THREE grades in one code \u2014 aluminium-alloy grade, chemical/silicone grade, and some upgraded-metallurgical (UMG) silicon \u2014 different markets and prices, one HS heading. Fine for a tonnage measure of silicon-metal ABSORPTION (all three are silicon metal being absorbed); NOT usable for any end-use claim, and the reason this can never be an A on HS purity. Ferrosilicon (720221/29) correctly excluded. Trade is hub-exposed (44% of flows, 23% of value via entrep\u00f4ts; Netherlands a top exporter) \u2014 but apparent consumption self-cancels transit, so hubs are annotated, not netted.",
